Z||000019||Shaun41178||11-01-2001||02:51 AM||||I used my nitrous all the time under 3000 rpm and had no problems.  ANd never boiled my tires either.  But I give credit to that due to the fact that I had an auto tranny just like Travis.  Autos are able to transfer the torque to the wheels better for acceleration then a manual as many manual drivers will just sit and spin them.  I was able to cut 1.8 60' times just like Travis by hitting it right off the line.  And I was doing this all with 215/60/14 street tires.<P>Joe it feels like an entirely different car.  Without the nitrous my car felt like a slug but with it I was hanging with the big v8 boys.  I am talking LS1's here.  Not top end but up to 70 or so I was right with them.  Auto tranny kept me from staying with them up top but for street racing it was awesome.  I only had mine jetted to a 70 hp shot.  You don't really feel the hp so much as you do the torque.  The amount of torque it adds is incredible.  I dynoed my car with a 50 shot of nitrous and it added over 65 lb-ft of torque to the rear wheels.  With that much exra torque off the line it was incredible how fast my car left others behind even if they got the jump on me.  I remember racing a 99 Mustang Cobra at the strip one day.  He cut an awesome light and got the quick jump but by the time the 60' line came around I was right next to him and by 330' I had about 1.5 cars on him.  If it was a street race he would have been embarressed to hell and back.  He ended up catching me at the end of the qtr and nosed me for the win but I had him up till 85 mph or so.<P>Most nitrous kits come with an arming switch and a switch you hook up to your TB.  When the TB goes wide open most FI cars go into a closed loop operation.  When this happens most cars go into a backup program to tell how much fuel to add and timing.  Depending on what kit you have the extra fuel is added from the stock injectors(dry kit) or through a fuel nozzle setup in the intake tract(wet kit)  I had a dry kit and I was quite happy with it.  I would go with a dry kit if you only plan on running a 70 or less.  Over that and I would go with a wet kit.  Wet kits have a tendency to puddle fuel in the intake tract and could ignite accidentally.  <P>Best way to go would be a direct port kit.  Joe I would recommend that if you do get nitrous.  Yes it would take a bit longer to install but much more equal distribution to the cylinders and you can set each cylinder to different amounts of nitrous if you want pending on how efficient they are.  Since you have that Electromotive unit I am sure you can monitor each individual cylinder of exhaust temps and base your nitrous needs of of those readings to get max power and safety.  Of course a simple dry kit will work well too as I had good luck with my dry kit.<P>For anyone thinking of nitrous that doesn't know how it works I would recommend going out and reading up on it instead of asking questions here.  Much more can be learned that way and it keeps the same questions from being asked over and over again.  Or go back and search the archives.  There are a lot of people here that want nitrous but don't even know how an internal combustion engine works.  To those people I say go pick up a book.  And no never install nitrous on an old engine or one that is not in its best health.  Especially if you don't have the money to rebuild it if it blows after the first time using it which can and has happened.  If you have the money and want to install nitrous for the sole purpose of blowing it up to have an excuse to rebuild it, then I say go for it.<P>Yes the 2.8 bottom end will hold up to nitrous.  I think Travis' car is a living testimony to that as well as my car since I had my kit on for some 3 odd years with no problems.  You just can't use too much nitrous and it has to be properly tuned.  That means dyno time if you want it done right.  <P>As to cost most cost around $500 for a kit.  Around $35 to refill the bottle when it goes empty.  Easy to install.  If you can put in a radio you can install a nitrous kit.<P>------------------<BR>Shaun 85 GT with nitrous<BR>0-60 in 5.6 seconds<BR>Shaun41178@hotmail.com<BR>PORN FOREVER!||||reg||1
Z||000020||1FST2M6||11-01-2001||08:39 AM||||shauns always so articulate and on the $..  .. Yeah what Shaun said..   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"> and for a link<P> <A HREF="http://www.nitrouswarehouse.com" TARGET=_blank>www.nitrouswarehouse.com</A>  20216 is the part # for the V6. it's $449. <P>------------------<BR>0-60 in 4.91<P> <A HREF="http://www.1fst2m6.com" TARGET=_blank>www.1fst2m6.com</A>  - underconstruction - but check it out anywho...||||reg||1
Z||000021||falconhulk||11-01-2001||11:59 AM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by 1FST2M6:<BR><B>shauns always so articulate and on the $..  .. Yeah what Shaun said..    <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"> and for a link<P> <A HREF="http://www.nitrouswarehouse.com" TARGET=_blank>www.nitrouswarehouse.com</A>  20216 is the part # for the V6. it's $449. <P></B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>With this kit do the stock injectors put out enough fuel at WOT for the nos?  <P>||||reg||1
Z||000022||Shaun41178||11-01-2001||12:34 PM||||Hehe thanks Travis.  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"><P>With the kit Travis is talking about it is a wet kit.  The stock injectors don't supply the extra fuel.  You tap into your fuel rail(I believe Travis just went off of the schrader valve) and plumb a fuel line to the intake manifold before the TB.  A nozzle comes with the kit that mixes the fuel and the nitrous togethor and sprays it into the intake manifold.  This is how the demands of extra fuel are met with a wet kit.<P>When using a dry kit a vacuum dingy thingie(how is that for articulate?) is plumbed into the stock factory vacuum hoses and closes off the FPR to keep fuel from going back to the fuel pump.  By doing this it effectively raises fuel pressure up around 75 psi or so at the rail and forces more fuel into through the stock injectors into the cylinders.<P>Hey Travis guess what I picked up?  A gen 3 engine complete minus harness and accesories.  Brand new!  And since everything is brand new I am going to try and put it all togethor in my aluminum block and throw it in a fiero.  Probably get new pistons however so I can have a nice clean overbore to start with and forged for nitrous use of course  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif">  Biggest prob will probably be the dogbone mount and alternator setup.  Should be a fun car to drive though.  hehe<P>||||reg||1
Z||000023||falconhulk||11-01-2001||02:01 PM||||Does the kit mentioned above come with all hoses needed to do this, or do you have to buy additional?  What about instructions/suggestions on where to do it? ||||reg||1
